---
import SectionIntro from "../ui/SectionIntro.astro";
---
<section class="section why-ter">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="services-head">
      <SectionIntro
        eyebrow="Why TER?"
        title="A shared architecture for economics"
        description="Economics already provides powerful theories of markets, information, strategic interaction, institutions, behavior, externalities, equilibrium, and dynamic change. TER brings many of these established mechanisms into a common agent-centered architecture for studying how decisions, interactions, outcomes, and economic change fit together."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="service-list">
      <article data-reveal>
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Markets and incentives</h3>
          <p>Scarcity, opportunity cost, prices, supply and demand, incentives, and equilibrium can be represented through agents pursuing objectives, facing constraints, selecting actions, and interacting with one another.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="tag">Markets</span>
      </article>

      <article data-reveal>
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Behavior and interaction</h3>
          <p>Bounded rationality, asymmetric information, strategic interaction, and externalities fit within the same architecture through differences in beliefs, perceived feasible actions, valuations, decision processes, and interactions.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="tag">Behavior</span>
      </article>

      <article data-reveal>
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Institutions and dynamics</h3>
          <p>Institutions, technological change, feedback, collective action, and adjustment can be studied through how they alter information, incentives, constraints, feasible actions, outcomes, and subsequent decisions.</p>
        </div>
        <span class="tag">Dynamics</span>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="closing-note lede" data-reveal>
      TER's central research claim is that many economic phenomena traditionally described through separate models and theories can be understood as different configurations and dynamics of a common agent-centered economic architecture.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .why-ter{
    background: var(--surface-2);
  }
  .services-head { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.3fr .7fr; gap: 3rem; align-items: end; }
  .service-list { margin-top: clamp(3rem,7vw,6rem); border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  article { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr auto; align-items: center; gap: 2rem; padding: 1.7rem .5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  article .number { color: var(--muted); font-size: .7rem; }
  article h3 { margin-bottom: .6rem; }
  article p { max-width: 42rem; margin: 0; color: var(--muted); }
  .tag { padding: .4rem .65rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 999px; color: var(--muted); font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 650; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .closing-note { padding-top: 2rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  @media (max-width: 800px) { .services-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } article { grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr; align-items: start; } article .tag { display: none; } }
</style>
